// Its own file, so the home page loads it on demand and it stays out of the first download.

/** Each passage's opening paragraph: the text before its last sentence, and that sentence. */
export const RC_LEADS: Record<string, [string, string]> = {
  "econ-cities": ["The standard account of urban growth treats the city as a bargain struck with open eyes. Workers accept dearer housing, longer commutes and dirtier air because wages in dense places run well above those elsewhere; firms accept the same bargain because proximity lowers the cost of finding workers, suppliers and customers. Economists call the benefit agglomeration and, having named it, tend to stop.","The label describes a correlation rather than a mechanism, and it conceals a question that matters for policy: whether the premium that cities pay is a reward for being there or a reward for being a certain kind of person who happens to be there."],
  "econ-thrift": ["Moralists have always found thrift easy to praise, and economists have found it hardly harder, so long as the thrifty are considered one at a time. A household that spends less than it earns acquires a cushion against misfortune; a country whose households all do so appears to acquire a fund from which factories can be built. It was against this comfortable symmetry that Keynes directed what later came to be called the paradox of thrift. Suppose that, alarmed by the prospect of unemployment, everyone tries to save more at once. One household's thrift is another household's lost custom. Shops sell less, payrolls shrink, and the incomes out of which the extra saving was to come contract with them.","The attempt to add to the nation's savings may leave them no larger than before; what was prudent in each case proves futile in the aggregate."],
  "econ-rules": ["The oldest argument for letting central bankers use their judgment is also the most persuasive: no formula composed in advance can anticipate the shocks an economy will deliver, and a rule that is sensible in ordinary times may prove perverse in extraordinary ones. Judgment adapts where rules cannot. Against this, economists who made their names in the 1970s raised an objection that has nothing to do with the intelligence of central bankers. A bank that can alter its course at will is, for that very reason, difficult to believe.","If it announces that it will hold inflation down, wage-setters have reason to suspect that it will weaken once they and their employers have committed themselves to contracts, because a burst of surprise inflation yields a temporary gain in employment that a tempted official may find hard to resist."],
  "econ-lemons": ["The market for used cars has become the standard illustration of what happens when one party to a transaction knows more than the other. The seller knows the history of the vehicle; the buyer can inspect only its paintwork. Wary, the buyer will pay no more than the price of an average car, and this offer insults the owners of better-than-average ones, who withdraw. The average quality of the cars still for sale then declines, the offer falls, and the process repeats. In the limit only the worst cars remain, and an exchange that would have profited sellers and buyers alike never takes place. When George Akerlof set out this argument in 1970, journals that thought it trivial turned it down.","The verdict has not worn well."],
  "econ-minwage": ["For most of the twentieth century the economics of the minimum wage was an application of a result so elementary that it was taught before most students had met a regression. A legal floor above the wage that employers would otherwise pay prices some workers out of jobs; the only open question was how many. The conclusion owed its authority less to evidence than to the coherence of the theory from which it came, and results that sat awkwardly with it were more often blamed on flaws in the data than on flaws in the theory.","A prediction that cannot be embarrassed by observation has ceased to be a prediction and become a presumption, and presumptions are what natural experiments exist to test."],
  "econ-commons": ["Garrett Hardin's 1968 essay on the commons has been read so often as a parable about herdsmen that its actual structure is easily forgotten. Hardin asked what happens when a resource is open to all and each user captures the whole of the gain from taking more while bearing only a fraction of the cost. His answer, that users will take until the resource is ruined, followed from the assumptions with the rigour of a theorem, and it would be wrong to think that Elinor Ostrom, whose work is usually offered as its refutation, ever disputed the logic. What she disputed was the generalisation that the theorem was made to bear: that shared resources must therefore be either privatised or placed under the control of the state.","The leap from 'can' to 'must', she suspected, was where the argument went astray."],
  "phil-theseus": ["The puzzle attributed to Plutarch concerns a ship preserved by the Athenians in commemoration of Theseus, whose timbers, as they decayed, were replaced one at a time until, it was said, not a plank of the original remained. Philosophers disagreed whether it was still the same vessel, and Hobbes, characteristically, sharpened the case by supposing that someone had gathered the discarded planks and reassembled them. There are now two rival claimants, one retaining the form and unbroken history of the original, the other retaining its matter.","The temptation is to treat the dispute as a factual one awaiting a sufficiently ingenious argument, as though the ship's identity were a hidden property that careful scrutiny might eventually disclose."],
  "phil-luck": ["Consider two drivers who, after the same convivial evening, make the same imprudent decision to drive home, and who are equally careless at the same bend. On one road a child steps out; on the other, nobody does. The first driver is a killer and the second merely a fortunate offender; the first will be censured, punished and perhaps haunted for life, while the second sleeps untroubled. Our reactions, and the law's, register the difference with confidence.","Yet the sole difference between the two lies in something neither driver governed, and this is precisely what a venerable principle of morality forbids us to treat as relevant."],
  "phil-experts": ["A moment's reflection discloses how little of what we believe we have verified for ourselves. That the earth orbits the sun, that tobacco promotes cancer, that a city I have never visited exists: none of these could I defend by producing the evidence, and in each I rely on testimony, on teachers, textbooks, physicians and the long chain of persons behind them. Philosophers in the empiricist tradition were inclined to regard such reliance as an intellectual concession, tolerable in the child but unworthy of the mature mind, which ought to resolve to believe nothing it has not examined.","The ideal is noble; it is also, in the conditions of modern knowledge, unattainable."],
  "psy-planning": ["Invited to forecast the duration of a task, people characteristically err on the side of brevity. Students underestimate the time their dissertations will consume, householders the expense of renovation, and governments the cost of bridges and railways, sometimes by multiples. Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky christened the tendency the planning fallacy: the disposition to predict that one's own undertakings will fare better than comparable undertakings have fared in the past. In one study, students asked when they would finish their theses predicted an average about three weeks shorter than the one they eventually took, and even their 'worst case' forecasts proved too sanguine.","What makes the phenomenon remarkable is not that people are ignorant of that past, but that they hold the relevant knowledge and fail to apply it."],
  "psy-memory": ["We are inclined to picture memory as a recording: an experience is deposited in the brain and, when we recall it, replayed, perhaps somewhat faded. A century of research has rendered this picture difficult to defend.","Remembering appears to resemble the reconstruction of a scene from fragments far more than the playing of a tape, the lacunae being filled by general knowledge, expectation and even the phrasing of the questions we are asked."],
  "psy-choice-blind": ["In a study published in 2005, researchers showed volunteers pairs of photographs of faces and asked them to select the more attractive in each pair. After every choice the experimenter slid the chosen card across the table and invited the volunteer to explain the preference. On some trials, by a simple sleight of hand, the volunteer was handed instead the face that had been rejected. Most of the time nobody noticed, although the faces lay side by side and the volunteers could look as long as they liked.","More striking still, volunteers went on to justify a preference for a face they had not chosen, sometimes citing features, such as earrings, which only the rejected face possessed."],
  "psy-willpower": ["In an experiment of the late 1990s, hungry volunteers were seated in a room redolent of freshly baked cookies. Some were permitted to eat the cookies; others were obliged to eat radishes and to leave the cookies untouched. All were then set a puzzle which, unknown to them, could not be solved. Those who had resisted the cookies abandoned the puzzle markedly sooner. The effect was large and the design easily copied, and the experimenters proposed that self-control draws upon a limited resource, depleted by one act of restraint and therefore diminished for the next.","For two decades the result was cited in textbooks, in policy papers and in the advice offered to dieters."],
  "phil-future": ["We commonly suppose that the unborn can be wronged. A government that entombs radioactive waste in a manner certain to breach containment in three centuries seems to act wickedly towards those who will then be living, although not one of them is alive to be injured today. Our legislation, our pension schemes and our conservation efforts all presuppose that those who come after us have claims upon us, and the presupposition seems too natural to need defence.","A great deal of what we believe we owe the future depends upon intuitions of this kind, and it is therefore disquieting that Derek Parfit, in the 1980s, showed them to rest on a foundation far less secure than they appear."],
  "phil-sentience": ["Until recently the animal-welfare statutes of many jurisdictions protected only vertebrates. Crabs, lobsters and octopuses lay beyond their reach, which is why a lobster might lawfully be boiled alive where the same treatment of a chicken would be a crime. The boundary was drawn less by evidence than by habit, and by the intractable difficulty of ascertaining what, if anything, such creatures feel.","It is worth observing that the difficulty is of a peculiar kind: it arises not from a missing instrument but from the absence of any agreed test."],
  "psy-anchoring": ["In a celebrated experiment of the 1970s, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman spun a wheel of fortune before their volunteers. It had been rigged to stop at either 10 or 65, and the volunteers, who watched it turn, knew that it bore no relation to what followed. They were asked whether the proportion of African countries in the United Nations was higher or lower than the number the wheel had shown, and then to estimate the true figure. Those who had seen 10 gave a median estimate of 25 per cent; those who had seen 65 gave 45.","A number known to be random had drawn their judgement toward itself."],
  "psy-marshmallow": ["In the late 1960s and early 1970s the psychologist Walter Mischel offered preschool children at a Stanford nursery a choice: they might eat one treat, such as a marshmallow, at once, or wait alone in a room for up to twenty minutes and receive two. The experiment's attraction lay in its austerity, a single child, a single sweet and a closed door. Years later Mischel and his colleagues followed up some of the children and reported that those who had waited longer went on to do better at school and to cope more effectively with stress in adolescence.","The test became a famous emblem of self-control and of the notion that the capacity to defer gratification in early childhood predicts success in later life."],
  "sci-shrinking": ["In 1847 the German biologist Carl Bergmann observed that, among closely related warm-blooded animals, the populations inhabiting colder regions tend to be larger. The reasoning usually attached to the pattern is geometrical: because volume increases faster than surface area as a body grows, a large animal sheds proportionally less heat than a small one and so enjoys an advantage in the cold, whereas in the heat a small body dissipates warmth more readily.","Bergmann's rule, as it came to be called, is a tendency and not a law, and it has numerous exceptions; but it holds widely enough, from deer to sparrows, to have invited a bold extrapolation."],
  "sci-wood": ["Beneath the forest floor the roots of most trees are enveloped in the threads of fungi. The association, called mycorrhiza, is thought to be as old as land plants themselves and is ordinarily advantageous to both parties: the fungus gathers water and minerals from the soil and passes a portion to the tree, which in exchange supplies the fungus with sugars manufactured by photosynthesis.","Because a single fungus may attach itself to several trees, and a single tree to several fungi, ecologists began in the 1990s to describe forests as joined by subterranean networks."],
  "sci-placebo": ["In a well-designed drug trial, some patients receive the drug and others a placebo, an inert substance made to look identical to it, and neither the patients nor the clinicians treating them know who has received which. The purpose of the arrangement is not merely to establish whether the drug works but to measure how much better it works than the bare experience of being treated. That second quantity is often surprisingly large, and much of the interest of the placebo lies in its size.","It attracted attention in 1955, when the anaesthetist Henry Beecher reported that roughly a third of patients obtained satisfactory relief from a placebo, a figure later criticised as too high."],
  "sci-microbiome": ["Each of us carries tens of trillions of microbes in the gut, a community whose cells roughly equal in number the human cells of the body and whose collective genes are said to outnumber our own by a wide margin. For most of medical history these organisms were regarded chiefly as a source of infection.","Over the past two decades, inexpensive genetic sequencing has allowed them to be catalogued in detail, and the results have altered how scientists think of the body, for the gut's inhabitants help to digest fibre, synthesise vitamins, educate the immune system and keep harmful species in check."],
  "sci-leapsecond": ["For most of history time was defined by the sky. A day was one turn of the Earth, and the second a fraction of that day, a definition that could not survive the discovery that the day is not constant. In 1967 metrologists severed this link, redefining the second by the vibrations of caesium atoms, which are far steadier than the planet's spin.","The best atomic clocks now keep time so exactly that they would drift by less than a second in many millions of years."],
  "sci-dinobirds": ["In 1861, only two years after Darwin published On the Origin of Species, quarrymen in Bavaria uncovered a fossil that might have been made to order for his theory. Archaeopteryx had the feathers and wings of a bird but also the teeth, clawed fingers and long bony tail of a small reptile. Only a dozen or so specimens have been found since, and each is treated as a treasure. Thomas Huxley, Darwin's most combative defender, soon remarked on the likeness between the skeletons of birds and those of small dinosaurs, and the new fossil seemed to confirm his suspicion.","The animal appeared to be caught midway between two groups, and it set off a debate about the origin of birds that lasted more than a century."],
  "sci-ozone": ["In 1985 three British scientists reported that the ozone layer above Antarctica was thinning dramatically each spring. Ozone high in the atmosphere absorbs most of the sun's ultraviolet radiation, which damages DNA and causes skin cancer and cataracts. The cause was soon traced to chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, gases used in refrigerators, aerosol sprays and foam, the danger of which two chemists, Mario Molina and Sherwood Rowland, had foreseen in 1974 and for which they shared a Nobel prize in 1995 with Paul Crutzen.","Stable and harmless at ground level, CFCs drift into the stratosphere, where ultraviolet light breaks them apart and releases chlorine, which destroys ozone."],
  "soc-algorithms": ["When a bank employs software to determine who receives a loan, or a municipality to determine where police should be dispatched, the decision is frequently defended as objective, a claim that carries peculiar authority in institutions anxious to appear dispassionate. A machine, the argument runs, harbours no prejudices; it applies identical rules to every case, unlike a human officer who may be fatigued, hurried or biased.","The defence contains a truth and conceals a confusion, and it is the relation between the two that repays examination."],
  "soc-remote": ["For most of history, where one lived largely determined the work one could do. The pandemic briefly loosened that connection: millions of office workers discovered that much of their job could be performed from a spare room, and some never returned. The shift prompted forecasts that remote work would disperse opportunity away from expensive cities, enabling a programmer in a small town to earn a metropolitan salary while paying a provincial rent.","Cities, after all, command higher wages partly because proximity breeds productivity; were proximity to cease to matter, that premium might be expected to erode."],
  "soc-repair": ["A generation ago a broken radio went to a repair shop. Today a broken phone, kettle or printer is more often discarded. The change is familiar to anyone over forty, and is frequently lamented as a decline in values, a culture preferring novelty to care.","The sentiment is sincere; the diagnosis it implies is, however, doubtful, and the economics of repair supplies a less moralising account."],
  "soc-cash": ["For much of the history of aid, the notion of simply handing money to poor people was regarded with suspicion. Donors feared that cash would be squandered on alcohol or tobacco, or that it would sap the willingness to work. Behind these fears lay an assumption, seldom avowed, that poverty reflects defective judgement as much as scarcity of money. Aid therefore commonly arrived in kind, as food, livestock, training or buildings selected by outsiders who believed they knew what the poor required.","The paternalism was seldom malicious, but it was paternalism nonetheless."],
  "soc-streets": ["In 1961 the writer and activist Jane Jacobs published a book that assailed the planners then rebuilding American cities. Their ideal was order: homes in one zone, offices in another and shops in a third, with broad roads and open lawns between towers. Many of the neighbourhoods Jacobs defended were classed as slums and marked for clearance. Jacobs, who lived on a busy street in Greenwich Village, contended that the planners' tidy vision destroyed the very qualities that made cities function.","Her prose was polemical and her method anecdotal, relying on what she saw from her window rather than on statistics, a stance that in her day made her something of an outsider."],
  "soc-corrections": ["In 2010 two political scientists reported a disquieting result. When people who held a false belief were shown a correction, some of them held the belief more firmly than before. The finding, soon dubbed the backfire effect, seemed to explain a great deal about politics and the internet: facts, it appeared, could not only fail to persuade but actively entrench error.","Journalists and fact-checkers began to wonder whether their labour was doing more harm than good, a dilemma especially acute for institutions whose purpose is to set the record straight."],
  "lit-translation": ["The Italian pun that makes the translator a traitor, traduttore, traditore, owes its longevity to a picture of language that few people examine. On that picture a text is a vessel for a meaning that exists independently of its words, and translation is the pouring of the contents into a second vessel; the best translator is the one who spills least. The picture explains why translations are so often judged by what they lack, and why the highest praise for one is that it does not read like a translation at all. It has the advantage of simplicity.","Its disadvantage is that it is not true of any text worth translating."],
  "lit-minds": ["Novels permit what life forbids: they let us overhear another mind. In conversation we infer what others think from what they say and do, and are often wrong; in fiction we are, if not told directly, given something so close to certainty that the difference rarely troubles us. The technique most closely associated with this privilege is free indirect style, brought to its fullest development by Jane Austen and Gustave Flaubert, though its roots are older and its uses have since multiplied.","It deserves examination, because the claims made for fiction as a school of sympathy frequently rest on a vague idea of what fiction actually does."],
  "lit-tragedy": ["Aristotle's contemporaries did not need to be told that tragedy gave pleasure; the puzzle they bequeathed us is why. In ordinary life we avoid suffering and seldom pay to watch it, yet for more than two thousand years audiences have watched kings blind themselves and mothers lose their children and gone home, by their own account, satisfied. The puzzle is sometimes called the paradox of tragedy, and the name conceals an assumption: that pleasure and pain are two feelings, one of which has somehow been smuggled into the theatre to compensate for the other.","Most attempted solutions have accepted the assumption and differed only about the compensation."],
  "hist-maps": ["J. B. Harley, the historian of cartography, argued in the 1980s that maps should be read less as mirrors of the world than as texts that carry the interests of their makers. The suggestion was resisted by those who valued the map precisely for its freedom from argument, but it has since become orthodoxy, perhaps too comfortably. To say that a map selects, simplifies and chooses a centre is to say what is true of every representation; the interesting question is when such choices become instruments of power, and by what means.","The history of European mapping of other continents offers a revealing, if complicated, answer."],
  "hist-print": ["It is a commonplace that the printing press changed everything. After Gutenberg began to print from movable type at Mainz around 1450, the story runs, books became cheap, literacy spread, the Reformation became irresistible and modern science became possible. Historians have spent forty years complicating the story, and it is worth asking whether they have replaced it with something better or merely with something more cautious.","The suspicion is warranted, because the most influential objections have a common structure: each takes a strong claim about the technology and shows that the outcome depended on something else."],
  "hist-cholera": ["In late August 1854 cholera struck the Soho district of London with extraordinary ferocity, and within ten days it had killed some five hundred people in a few streets. The disease was dreaded for its speed, which could kill a healthy adult within a day. Medical opinion held that it was carried by miasma, a poisonous exhalation of rotting matter, an idea with a respectable pedigree and a good deal of circumstantial support: cholera struck hardest in the poorest, dirtiest and lowest-lying districts, and sanitary campaigns directed against filth had reduced other diseases.","The physician John Snow thought otherwise, holding that cholera was spread through water contaminated with the discharges of the sick, an idea he had advanced some years earlier on the strength of an earlier epidemic."],
  "hist-silk": ["The phrase 'Silk Road' was coined in 1877 by the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen, who had spent years in China surveying for the railways that he hoped would one day follow the old routes. It acquired its present glamour only in the twentieth century, through travel narratives, museum exhibitions and then television. The name now calls up a picture of caravans carrying bolts of silk from Chang'an to Rome, and the picture is a powerful one.","It is also, in its main outline, misleading, and the ways in which it misleads are more interesting than the correction that follows."],
  "lit-oral": ["For centuries scholars were puzzled by how one poet could have composed the Iliad and the Odyssey, poems of more than twenty-seven thousand lines between them, without the aid of writing. Some concluded that no single poet had done so, and that the poems were assembled by editors from shorter songs. The problem was recast in the 1930s by Milman Parry, a young American classicist, who had noticed that Homer's verse is built from repeated phrases, 'rosy-fingered dawn' or 'swift-footed Achilles', each of which fits a particular place in the line's metre.","Such phrases, he argued, were not decorations but tools, and a poet who possessed a sufficient stock of them could compose at speed without memorising."],
  "lit-detective": ["In 1929 the priest and crime writer Ronald Knox set down ten commandments for detective fiction. The culprit must be mentioned early; no more than one secret passage is permitted; the detective must not commit the crime; and no clue may be withheld from the reader. Knox wrote partly in jest, but his list recorded a real ambition of the period. A detective story, in what is now called its golden age, was to be a fair contest between author and reader, in which a careful reader could in principle name the culprit before the detective did.","The claim is usually accepted at face value, and it is worth noticing how often the books honour it only in the letter: the fairness consisted largely in the display of clues, and the conclusions drawn from them could depend on knowledge of an unusual poison, an obscure railway timetable or the habits of a particular social class."],
  "hist-calendar": ["The calendar introduced by Julius Caesar in 46 BCE assumed a year of exactly 365 and a quarter days, and added a leap day every fourth year to keep pace. The true solar year is about eleven minutes shorter. The error is trivial in a lifetime and considerable in a millennium: by the sixteenth century the calendar had drifted some ten days from the seasons, and the spring equinox, which the Church used to fix the date of Easter, fell around 11 March rather than the 21 March on which the calculations assumed it.","A discrepancy of this kind offended astronomers, but it was the Church's concern with Easter that turned it into a matter of state."],
  "arts-perspective": ["In the early fifteenth century Florentine painters acquired a technique that was to govern, for some five hundred years, what it meant for a picture to look correct. Linear perspective, associated with the architect Filippo Brunelleschi and set out in treatise form by Leon Battista Alberti in the 1430s, prescribes that parallel lines receding from the spectator converge upon a single vanishing point, so that a flat panel may simulate a window onto a measured space.","The technique is commonly narrated as a discovery: painters, after centuries of groping, at last learned to render the world as it really appears."],
  "arts-halls": ["Concert halls are among the few buildings whose quality is judged by ear, yet the judgement has proved remarkably elusive. Until the late nineteenth century acoustics was a matter of lore and happenstance: halls that sounded splendid were copied, those that did not were quietly altered or abandoned, and nobody could say with confidence why. The turning point is conventionally dated to 1895, when Wallace Sabine, a young Harvard physicist, was asked to remedy the muddy acoustics of a lecture theatre. By timing how long sound lingered in rooms, he established that reverberation depends upon a room's volume and upon the absorbing materials within it, and he reduced the relation to a formula.","Boston Symphony Hall, opened in 1900, was the first hall designed with its help."],
  "arts-forgery": ["In 1937 a Rotterdam museum bought a large biblical canvas, the Supper at Emmaus, after it had been warmly endorsed by Abraham Bredius, the most eminent Vermeer scholar of his day. Vermeer's surviving output is tiny, some three dozen pictures, and scholars had long hoped that a lost cache of early works would come to light. After the Second World War the canvas's true author, the Dutch painter Han van Meegeren, was arrested for selling a supposed national treasure to the Nazi leader Hermann Göring, and in his defence he took the unlikely course of confessing that the Vermeers were his own forgeries. To convince the sceptical he painted another before witnesses. Once the truth was known, critics who had hailed the canvases as sublime began to find them crude. Nothing about the paint had altered.","Why should knowledge of a picture's origin change our estimate of it?"],
  "arts-camera": ["When Louis Daguerre announced his photographic process in 1839, the French painter Paul Delaroche is supposed to have declared that 'from today painting is dead'. The remark is probably apocryphal, but the sentiment it expresses has long underwritten a familiar story. Painting, according to the story, had existed chiefly to record appearances; the camera did so more quickly and exactly; painters, relieved of the burden of resemblance, were therefore liberated to pursue colour, feeling and finally abstraction. Impressionism, Expressionism and the rest are thus presented as the dividend of a technological accident.","It is the kind of explanation that flatters both machinery and art, and it has been repeated in textbooks for generations."],
  "arts-montage": ["Film-makers have long believed in montage: the meaning of a shot, it is said, depends less on what it contains than on what precedes and follows it. The founding illustration is an experiment attributed to the Soviet director Lev Kuleshov, who taught at the Moscow film school and regarded editing as the essence of cinema. A shot of an actor's expressionless face, so the story runs, was alternated with images of a bowl of soup, a child in a coffin and a woman on a divan; audiences praised the actor's subtle rendering of hunger, grief and desire, though the face had not changed.","The anecdote has been retold in countless textbooks as proof that the viewer, rather than the performer, makes the emotion."],
  "tech-opacity": ["As machine-learning systems have come to influence who is hired, who is granted credit and who is judged likely to reoffend, a demand has gathered force that no consequential decision about a person should be left to a system unable to explain itself. The intuition behind the demand is respectable. A person refused a mortgage is ordinarily owed reasons, since reasons permit her to correct an error in the record, to contest a criterion she considers unjust, or at least to learn what she might alter in future.","A model whose internal workings are too intricate for even its designers to narrate appears to withhold precisely this courtesy, and legislators in several jurisdictions have accordingly proposed that individuals should enjoy a right to an explanation of any automated verdict that affects them."],
  "tech-training": ["Generative systems that compose prose, images and music are built by exposing a model to vast quantities of existing work, much of it protected by copyright and made by people who were neither consulted nor paid. Many creators regard this as appropriation on an industrial scale. Developers and their defenders reply by analogy. No writer has composed a sentence without having read thousands of others, and no painter has acquired a manner without studying the masters; creative practice is, on this view, a process of absorption and recombination, in which the notion of a wholly original work is a romantic fiction.","To permit a person to learn from the accumulated stock of culture, but to forbid a machine to do likewise, seems an arbitrary discrimination, one that rests more on sentiment about what machines are than on any principle about what learning is."],
  "tech-automation": ["Aviation offers an instructive history of automation. As autopilots grew capable of handling the greater part of a flight, the pilot's role was redefined from controlling the aircraft to monitoring the machine that controlled it. The change brought undoubted gains in safety, and accidents attributable to pilot error became markedly less frequent. Yet investigators began to notice an uncomfortable pattern in the accidents that remained: when the automation failed, or met conditions beyond those for which it had been designed, it handed control back to the crew at the moment of greatest difficulty, and the crew, deprived of routine practice, proved less able to cope than their predecessors would have been.","The more dependable the system, the argument runs, the more rarely do its human supervisors exercise the very skills which they will occasionally need most urgently."],
  "tech-pacing": ["In 1980 the technology theorist David Collingridge formulated a dilemma that has acquired fresh urgency with the arrival of general-purpose artificial intelligence. When a technology is young, he observed, it is still easy to shape, but its consequences are unknown, so that there is nothing definite to regulate against; when its harms have at last become plain, it has become so woven into economic and social life that control is expensive, slow and fiercely resisted. Regulators thus appear condemned either to act blindly or to act too late.","The predicament is sharpened by the speed of development, which has led some commentators to speak of a 'pacing problem': technology, they say, advances geometrically while law proceeds by committee, and the distance between the two widens with every year."],
  "tech-evidence": ["Public alarm about synthetic media has centred on a simple fear: that convincing forgeries of speech and footage, produced cheaply by anyone, will lead people to believe things that never happened. The fear is not groundless, but it may direct attention to the lesser danger. A forgery that is believed can be exposed, and often is, once it has been examined with care. The subtler harm lies in the reverse effect. When it is widely known that any recording can be fabricated, a person caught on genuine footage acquires a ready defence: that it is a fake. Legal scholars have called the resulting advantage the liar's dividend. It accrues to the guilty, and it is paid in the currency of the public's weakened confidence in everything that it sees and hears.","Where fakery is easy and denial costless, the burden of proof quietly shifts to whoever offers the truth."],
};
